  place so the assumed backend contract can be swapped when [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is real.

  [1mA. Identity store injected as the third parameter[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, identity)[39m. [38;5;153mlogin[39m awaits the server, then
  calls [38;5;153midentity.set(userId)[39m. You get a literal third parameter, and because the store arrives as an argument, [38;5;153mlogin[39m
  can be tested with a fake store and no browser. The cost is that the parameter is a collaborator, not the user's ID —
  it satisfies the letter of the request by changing what the parameter means.

  [1mB. Singleton import, identity as return value[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m imports [38;5;153midentity[39m directly and returns [38;5;153m{ success, userId }[39m. The
  least code and the most conventional shape for a small app. But [38;5;153mlogin[39m has no new parameter at all, so it doesn't
  answer your request as phrased, and the store becomes a hidden dependency that tests can't substitute without module
  mocking.

  [1mC. Event-driven store with subscribers[0m — the store is an [38;5;153mEventTarget[39m; [38;5;153mlogin[39m sets identity and the store notifies
  listeners, with a [38;5;153mstorage[39m-event bridge so a login in one tab updates the others. This is the one that actually
  delivers "other forms will need it later" without each form polling. It's also the most machinery for an app that
  currently has exactly one form and one consumer.

  I recommend [1mA[0m, with C's subscription deliberately deferred. A gives you the parameter you asked for in the only form
  that's coherent with a server-issued ID, keeps [38;5;153mlogin[39m testable without a DOM, and leaves the store's interface
  unchanged if you later add C's events — subscribers can be layered onto the same module without touching callers. C is
  the right destination, but building a notification system for one consumer is the part I'd expect to regret.
